// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  CHANNELS — P9.1 (#2935)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  GAP-09 multi-file schema: new domain file. Generator + datasource
//  remain in base.prisma.
//
//  ── SCHEMA ONLY ──
//
//  P9's standing rule, inherited from P8 and P5: tables ship one release
//  before the code that writes them. So this file lands five models, their
//  RLS and their encryption classification, and NO route, usecase, UI or
//  flag. #2795 (P8.1) and #1553 (P5.1) are the worked precedents.
//
//  ── WHY TENANTLESS, which is a PRIOR decision and not a new one ──
//
//  `social.prisma`'s own preamble already commits a channel to this, in
//  terms: "the social surfaces it gates are person-scoped rather than
//  farm-scoped — a profile, a direct message and A CHANNEL do not belong to
//  one farm. So there is no `tenantId` here, and the RLS classification is
//  deliberately not the tenant-scoped one."
//
//  A crop channel spans every farm growing that crop; an oblast channel
//  spans every farm in that oblast. A `tenantId` would make the channel a
//  farm's private room, which is the opposite of the feature.
//
//  Its own FILE rather than more of `social.prisma` for the reason
//  `public-profile.prisma` is its own file: that one is already 809 lines
//  over sixteen models spanning flags, trust-and-safety and direct
//  messages. The tenantlessness argument is CITED above rather than copied,
//  because two copies of a design decision is how they come to disagree.
//
//  ── THE SESSION VARIABLE, and picking wrong fails SILENTLY ──
//
//  Every policy here reads `app.user_id`, which ONLY `runInUserContext`
//  sets. It is NOT `app.actor_user_id`, which `runInTenantContext` sets
//  alongside a tenant.
//
//  This is #1664 and it cost a whole feature: `UserBlock`'s SELECT policy
//  keyed on `app.user_id` while the enforcement ran under
//  `app.actor_user_id`, so the block predicates selected from an empty view
//  and returned `false` for every pair, always. Blocking somebody had no
//  effect on any path and NOTHING ERRORED — an unset session variable
//  yields NULL and the arm matches nothing.
//
//  So the integration test runs in the context the PRODUCTION caller will
//  run in, not merely in one that makes the policy pass.
//
//  ── A POLICY ON A TABLE MAY NOT QUERY THAT TABLE ──
//
//  P8.1 learned this expensively: a WITH CHECK on `DirectMessage` that read
//  `DirectMessage` produced `ERROR: infinite recursion detected in policy
//  for relation "DirectMessage"` and refused EVERY insert, whatever the
//  command.
//
//  A channel is where that bites hardest, because the natural membership
//  rule is exactly that shape — "you may read the members if you are a
//  member" wants `ChannelMembership`'s policy to query `ChannelMembership`.
//
//  So `channel_membership_select` admits the caller's OWN ROW ONLY, exactly
//  as `conversation_participant_select` does, and `Channel.memberCount` is
//  DENORMALISED because a member cannot count rows they cannot see. Posts
//  and reactions may query `ChannelMembership` freely: that is a DIFFERENT
//  table from the one being guarded, so no recursion arises.
//
//  ── A CHANNEL IS PUBLIC, which is the real difference from a conversation ──
//
//  `conversation_select` admits participants only. A channel must be
//  readable by somebody who has NOT joined, or P9.2 cannot suggest one and
//  nobody can look before joining. So `channel_select` admits any
//  authenticated caller, and the gate is on POSTING rather than on reading.
//
//  Consequence worth stating once: there is no private channel in this
//  design. Adding one later means a visibility column plus a second SELECT
//  arm, not a change to these policies.
//

/// What a channel is ABOUT. Decides how P9.2 suggests it: a crop channel from
/// the farm's crops, an oblast channel from its location, a topic channel from
/// neither — which is why `TOPIC` exists rather than being a null `kind`.
enum ChannelKind {
  CROP
  OBLAST
  TOPIC
}

/// A post's kind. `QUESTION` is «Въпрос» and is the only kind `resolvedAt` may
/// be set on; `CARD` is P9.4's generated content, which shares the feed and so
/// shares the table — it is moderatable and reportable for free that way.
enum ChannelPostKind {
  POST
  QUESTION
  CARD
}

/// A topic channel. PUBLIC and discoverable — see the header.
model Channel {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// The topic key, e.g. `crop:wheat`, `oblast:BG-16`, `topic:subsidies`.
  ///
  /// UNIQUE, and that is what makes P9.2's seed idempotent BY CONSTRUCTION
  /// rather than by a `seeded` flag somebody forgets to set — the
  /// `NoticeScore.reportId` precedent (#1596). It also makes suggestion a JOIN
  /// rather than a string match: an oblast channel's key carries the same
  /// `BG-16` code `ExchangeListing.oblast` stores.
  key String @unique

  /// Both locales, in the schema, because a channel NAME is reference data
  /// seeded by `dist/seed.mjs` rather than UI copy in `messages/`. A seeded
  /// row cannot reach a `next-intl` catalogue.
  nameBg String
  nameEn String

  descriptionBg String?
  descriptionEn String?

  kind ChannelKind

  /// DENORMALISED, and not an optimisation: `channel_membership_select` admits
  /// the caller's own row only (the recursion rule in the header), so a member
  /// physically cannot count the others. Maintained by the usecase that joins
  /// and leaves, in the same transaction as the membership write.
  memberCount Int @default(0)

  /// «Slow mode» — P9.3. Seconds a member must wait between posts; 0 is off.
  ///
  /// Seconds rather than a boolean because P5.5a's rule is that slow mode is a
  /// reduced BUDGET and the per-channel knob is the operator's. A column rather
  /// than a constant so a channel that is being flooded can be slowed without a
  /// deploy — the same reasoning that makes feature flags a table.
  slowModeSeconds Int @default(0)

  /// Archived channels stay readable and stop accepting posts. A delete would
  /// take every post with it, and a channel's history is the reason anybody
  /// joined it.
  archivedAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  memberships ChannelMembership[]
  posts       ChannelPost[]

  /// P9.2 lists by kind to build the suggestion sets.
  @@index([kind])
  /// The feed's own ordering, for a channel directory sorted by activity.
  @@index([archivedAt, memberCount])
}

/// One person's membership of one channel.
model ChannelMembership {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  channelId String
  /// No relation to `User`: these models are tenantless and person-scoped, and
  /// `DirectMessage.senderUserId` is the precedent — a bare id, so a channel
  /// never needs a tenant context to resolve its author.
  userId String

  joinedAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// P9.3's ban. The ROW SURVIVES a ban deliberately: deleting it would let the
  /// banned person rejoin and start posting again, and `@@unique([channelId,
  /// userId])` means a rejoin cannot create a second row to escape through.
  ///
  /// `channel_post_insert` requires this to be NULL, so the ban is enforced in
  /// SQL and not only in a usecase — the layer a refactor removes.
  bannedAt DateTime?

  /// The read cursor. The caller's own act on their own row, which is why
  /// `channel_membership_update` exists at all.
  lastReadAt DateTime?

  /// Muted: still a member, no push. P9.3 pushes only for mentions and replies,
  /// so this narrows an already-narrow trigger.
  mutedAt DateTime?

  channel Channel @relation(fields: [channelId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The identity, and the ban's enforcement: one row per person per channel.
  @@unique([channelId, userId])
  /// "which channels am I in" — the inbox-side read, and the FK index layer B
  /// requires for `userId` not leading a composite.
  @@index([userId])
}

/// A post in a channel. `body` is PLAINTEXT — see below.
model ChannelPost {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  channelId String

  /// The author, as a bare id. A forged author is refused by
  /// `channel_post_insert`'s WITH CHECK, which pins it to the session variable.
  authorUserId String

  /// PLAINTEXT, and this is a DEPARTURE from both message models in this repo.
  ///
  /// `DirectMessage.body` and `ExchangeMessage.body` are encrypted under the
  /// global KEK, because a two-party row under either party's DEK is unreadable
  /// by the other. A channel post is different in KIND: it is addressed to a
  /// group, intended to be widely read, and P9.7 requires it SEARCHABLE.
  /// Encrypting it would make search impossible without a second plaintext
  /// copy, which is worse than not encrypting — the copy would be the real
  /// store and nothing would guard it.
  ///
  /// So `ChannelPost.body` carries a `DELIBERATELY_PLAINTEXT` entry keyed
  /// `ChannelPost.body`. That entry is REQUIRED, not optional: `body` is a
  /// field name the manifest carries for other models, and since #1222 the
  /// manifest is per `(model, field)` — so an unclassified model no longer
  /// resolves to `'*'` and the guard refuses the silence.
  ///
  /// It is still SANITISED at the usecase (C.5), where
  /// `sanitize-rich-text-coverage` verifies the field BY NAME. There is no
  /// write path in a schema-only PR, so `RICH_TEXT_COVERAGE` gains its entry in
  /// the PR that adds the writer — exactly as P8.6's link-card columns had to
  /// (#2800).
  body String

  kind ChannelPostKind @default(POST)

  /// «Решено» — P9.3. Only meaningful on a `QUESTION`; the usecase enforces
  /// that, because a CHECK naming an enum value would have to be rewritten
  /// every time the enum grows.
  resolvedAt DateTime?

  /// A thread reply. NULL for a top-level post.
  ///
  /// Self-referential rather than a separate `ChannelThread`: a thread here is
  /// one level deep, so a parent pointer says everything a table would and the
  /// feed query stays one read. P9.3's «Също изпрати в канала» is a reply that
  /// ALSO gets a top-level copy, which is two rows and not a flag.
  parentPostId String?
  parentPost   ChannelPost?  @relation("ChannelPostThread", fields: [parentPostId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  replies      ChannelPost[] @relation("ChannelPostThread")

  /// A MODERATOR's removal (P9.3). A nullable timestamp rather than a delete,
  /// because an Art 17 statement of reasons carries redress and #2867 built the
  /// restore route that clears exactly this shape of column.
  removedByModerationAt DateTime?

  /// P9.3's auto-hide after three reports from verified farms. SEPARATE from
  /// `removedByModerationAt` on purpose: one is a human decision and the other
  /// is a threshold, they are reversed by different acts, and collapsing them
  /// would make a statement of reasons claim a moderator looked when none did.
  autoHiddenAt DateTime?

  /// The AUTHOR's own retraction, distinct from both of the above. A reader is
  /// told «изтрито»; a moderator's removal and an auto-hide are not the
  /// author's doing and must not read as theirs.
  deletedAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  channel   Channel               @relation(fields: [channelId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  reactions ChannelPostReaction[]
  mentions  ChannelPostMention[]

  /// The feed: a channel's top-level posts, newest first.
  @@index([channelId, createdAt])
  /// A thread's replies.
  @@index([parentPostId])
  /// "my posts", and the FK index layer B requires.
  @@index([authorUserId])
}

/// One reaction by one person to one post.
model ChannelPostReaction {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  postId String
  userId String

  /// The emoji itself. Bounded by the usecase to the agreed set; a column
  /// rather than an enum because the set is product copy and an enum change is
  /// a migration plus an OpenAPI narrowing — #2868 is what that costs.
  emoji String

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  post ChannelPost @relation(fields: [postId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// One reaction per person per post per emoji.
  @@unique([postId, userId, emoji])
  @@index([userId])
}

/// A person mentioned in a post. A TABLE, not a derived list.
///
/// P9.3 caps mentions at five, and a cap is only enforceable if mentions are a
/// constrained thing. Derived from the body text at render time, the cap would
/// be advisory and a post could mention a hundred people; as rows, the usecase
/// counts them before inserting and the count is checkable in SQL.
///
/// It is also what makes the mention PUSH addressable without parsing prose in
/// the notification path — P9.3 pushes only for mentions and replies.
model ChannelPostMention {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  postId String
  /// The mentioned person.
  userId String

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  post ChannelPost @relation(fields: [postId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// One mention of one person per post — so a body naming somebody twice
  /// cannot push twice.
  @@unique([postId, userId])
  /// "where was I mentioned" — the «Активност» read (P9.7).
  @@index([userId])
}
